10:15:38 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: Governments and central banks usually do **not** know the exact location of every banknote or coin at every moment. What they can measure very accurately is the **total amount they have issued and not yet removed from circulation**. In simplified form: **Currency in circulation = currency issued − currency returned and destroyed/withdrawn** For example, a central bank records: - How many notes and coins it prints or mints - How much it sends to commercial banks - How much cash commercial banks return - How many old or damaged notes it destroys - How much cash remains in central-bank vaults This produces a very reliable accounting total, often reported as “currency in circulation.” However, they generally cannot know exactly: - Who currently holds each note - Whether it is in someone’s wallet, a shop’s till, or a bank ATM - How much domestic currency is being held abroad - How much has been lost, destroyed, or hidden - How much counterfeit cash exists Banknotes have serial numbers, but most countries do not track each individual note’s movements in real time. Serial numbers are mainly useful for anti-counterfeiting, investigations, and production control. So the central bank knows the **official net amount of genuine cash outstanding** very closely, but not the precise real-world whereabouts—or even continued existence—of every physical note and coin.
