10:15:45 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Yes—at the production and central-bank level, that is basically correct. A government or central bank keeps strict records of: - The number and denomination of notes printed - The number of coins minted - Serial-number ranges assigned to batches of notes - Cash shipped to banks and cash-processing facilities - Cash returned, destroyed, or held in official vaults So it can account for the total value of authorized currency it has created and, after subtracting retired currency, estimate the official amount still outstanding. The important distinction is that “tracking every dollar printed” does **not** usually mean continuously tracking each physical bill after it enters public use. A $20 bill’s serial number exists, but the central bank normally does not receive a real-time report every time that specific bill changes hands. It is more like inventory accounting: 1. Print or mint cash. 2. Record its value and serial-number batch. 3. Send it into the banking system. 4. Receive some cash back over time. 5. Remove damaged, worn, or suspect notes. That lets authorities know the official total very well, while the physical location and owner of individual bills are mostly unknown.
