10:16:01 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That analogy is mostly right. A central bank can know the **exact accounting amount of official currency it has issued and not recorded as destroyed or returned**. In that sense, it knows its “inventory outstanding” very precisely. For example: - It prints $1 billion in notes. - $100 million is still in central-bank vaults. - $50 million has been returned worn out and destroyed. Then it can report roughly $850 million as currency outstanding/in circulation. But the number is not necessarily the exact amount of usable physical cash actually “out there,” because some issued notes may have been: - Lost - Accidentally destroyed, such as in fires or floods - Taken permanently out of the country - Hoarded and never returned - Counterfeited Those genuine lost or destroyed notes remain in the central bank’s accounting total until they eventually return—if they ever do. And counterfeit notes are not in the official total at all. So: - **Exact official outstanding amount:** yes, essentially. - **Exact amount physically existing and actively circulating among people:** no, not exactly. Also, published figures can be delayed or rounded, but the central bank’s internal issuance and withdrawal records are highly precise.
